gm-method/rendering ROOT CAUSE (a @three.ez/instanced-mesh InstancedMesh2 with addLOD levels): enabling per-instance front-to-back sorting (m.sortObjects=true + m.customSort=createRadixSort(m)) is INCOMPATIBLE with the lib's LOD level-assignment and silently DROPS every instance that enters the nearest LOD band (distance < the first addLOD cutover D1). The sort branch of frustumCullingLOD walks the depth-sorted render list bumping the level index monotonically against levels[k].distance; an instance in the [0,D1) band fails to be placed in any level's drawn index array, so it renders in NO bucket and VANISHES while still active+visible+in-frustum. Symptom: ALL instanced LOD objects disappear when the camera approaches, at the SAME distance (= the LOD0/LOD1 cutover), over clear ground, not occlusion. FIX: do not set sortObjects/customSort while LODs are in use; the non-sort BVHCullingLOD per-instance path assigns the nearest band (LOD0) correctly. DECISIVE WITNESS TECHNIQUE that cracked it after many dead ends: a per-instance 'actualBucket' probe -- scan each LOD level's object.instanceIndex.array over its first count[L] entries for the target instance id; actualBucket=-1 means assigned-to-no-drawn-bucket (the true vanish), which is STRONGER than reading LODinfo.render.count (species totals) or getObjectLODIndexForDistance (intended mapping, not actual). Toggling sortObjects on/off and re-reading actualBucket isolates the cause in one run. GENERAL: when an instanced-LOD thing vanishes 'when close at a fixed distance', suspect the LOD bucket ASSIGNMENT (sort vs non-sort path), not the geometry, cull volume, depth, or near-plane -- and prove it by which index array actually contains the instance, not by aggregate counts.